$\cot 10 + \tan 5 = 0$ gab. This solution takes advantage of the reciprocal identity $\cot x = \frac{1}{\tan x}$ and the tangent half-angle identity $\tan (x/2) = \frac{\sin x}{1 + \cos x}.$ First, we rewrite the summation of a cotangent and a tangent in terms of a cotangent and a sine: \begin{align*}
\cot 10 + \tan 5 &= \frac{1}{\tan 10} + \tan 5 \\
&= \frac{1}{\tan 10} + \frac{\sin 5}{1 + \cos 5} \\
&= \frac{(\sin 5)(1 + \cos 5)}{\sin 5 \cdot (1 + \cos 5)}. \end{align*}

Observe that the numerator and denominator are equal, so the expression reduces to $1.$ Thus, $\cot 10 + \tan 5 = \boxed{1}.$

